] Verify the actual current os.kill(pid, 0) behavior on Windows (signal 0 is unsupported/raises, not a clean TerminateProcess) and specify the Windows liveness probe accordingly rather than deriving it from the 'terminates the target' premise.","cohort":["T1"],"criteria":["T1"],"decision":"advisory","decision_margin":-0.105,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan: 'os.kill(pid, 0), which on Windows is documented to TerminateProcess the target rather than probe it'","GitHub browser-use#4352: '_is_process_alive(1234) # SystemError: os.kill doesn't support signal 0 on Windows'","GitHub cpython#125298: 'Behaviour of os.kill() on Windows may be misunderstood' — signal-value dependent handling","lock_owner.py:288-299 confirms _pid_alive relies on os.kill(pid, 0) and treats non-ProcessLookupError OSError as 'alive'"],"finding":"The plan asserts as fact that os.kill(pid, 0) 'on Windows is documented to TerminateProcess the target rather than probe it', but current CPython behavior is that os.kill with signal 0 on Windows is unsupported and raises (e.g. 'os.kill doesn't support signal 0 on Windows') rather than performing a clean terminate; the special TerminateProcess path applies to non-CTRL signal values. The corrective action (narrow the liveness probe on Windows) is sound, but the underlying capability characterization is imprecise and should be resolved against the current os.kill contract so the chosen Windows liveness probe is correct.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"fa8c32c475f2c778b","impact":0.85,"is_primary":null,"location":"What a correct fix requires, item 3; AC 'lock_owner._pid_alive never calls os.kill on a platform where that terminates the target'","norm_id":"n565ade983f46722e","priority":0.595,"reason":"default-advisory","recalled":false,"scenarios":["Author implements the Windows liveness narrowing based on the 'TerminateProcess for signal 0' premise; if the real behavior is a raised error, _pid_alive's existing 'OSError -> alive' branch would treat every Windows pid as alive, silently disabling reclamation on Windows rather than narrowing it correctly."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"","tier":"LLM","validity":0.7,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"na","asserted_capability_confirmed":"yes","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"no","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"yes","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"yes","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"no"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"contradicts_reality","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"medium","prod_impact":"low","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"self_revealing","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.9,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Verify the os.kill(pid, 0)-on-Windows terminate-vs-probe claim against CPython docs/source (cite it) and add an AC/test that empirically confirms the chosen _pid_alive narrowing on windows-latest rather than relying on the asserted behavior.","cohort":["T3"],"criteria":["T3"],"decision":"advisory","decision_margin":-0.305,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan quote: 'os.kill(pid, 0), which on Windows is documented to TerminateProcess the target rather than probe it'","lock_owner._pid_alive at src/rebar/_store/lock_owner.py:288-299 uses os.kill(pid, 0) and treats non-ProcessLookupError as alive — its docstring assumes POSIX signal-0 probe semantics","EVIDENCE CLASS: Unverified — the Windows os.kill behavior is stated from documentation recall, not confirmed against CPython docs/source in this review"],"finding":"Requirement 3 makes a load-bearing external-behavior assertion — that os.kill(pid, 0) on Windows 'is documented to TerminateProcess the target rather than probe it' — without a verification citation. This drives the design decision to narrow the liveness probe on Windows, so its correctness is material, yet it is asserted rather than verified.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f1c568a4f2938bfc0","impact":0.85,"is_primary":null,"location":"Section 'What a correct fix requires', requirement 3","norm_id":"nab1b3c506c72c18e","priority":0.595,"reason":"default-advisory","recalled":false,"scenarios":["If os.kill(pid, 0) on Windows does NOT terminate for sig=0 (or raises differently), the plan narrows a probe that did not need narrowing, or conversely under-narrows and reclamation kills a live holder","The windows-latest native test in AC4 covers the lock leg but not necessarily the _pid_alive/os.kill terminate-on-Windows claim"],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"","tier":"LLM","validity":0.7,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"na","asserted_capability_confirmed":"yes","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"no","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"yes","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"yes","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"no"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"contradicts_reality","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"medium","prod_impact":"low","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"self_revealing","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":null,"blocking_enabled":null,"checklist_item":"","cohort":null,"criteria":["P6"],"decision":"advisory","decision_margin":null,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["verify command `grep -nE '(^|[^_[:alnum:]])fcntl\\.' src/rebar/_store/lock.py src/rebar/_store/lock_owner.py src/rebar/_commands/doctor_locks.py` inspects output with `grep` but asserts nothing — add an exit-code or comparison (-q, [ \"$(...)\" -eq N ], jq -e) so a failure is observable"],"finding":"Acceptance-criteria / definition-of-done quality issues.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f607dd457c67f9209","impact":0.4,"is_primary":null,"location":"","norm_id":"ned06ebc801a522e3","priority":0.4,"reason":null,"recalled":false,"scenarios":[],"severity":"minor","suggested_fix":"Split compound criteria, replace subjective terms with observable outcomes, and state how each is verified.","tier":"DET","validity":1.0,"verification":null},{"block_threshold":0.7,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Resolve the msvcrt/LockFileEx release-on-process-death timing guarantee against Microsoft's docs (release is not documented as instantaneous) and state how the Windows staleness proof and the native AC5 test tolerate release latency.","cohort":["T1"],"criteria":["T1"],"decision":"advisory","decision_margin":-0.3425,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan: 'kernel-mediated, dies-with-the-process exclusivity flock provides'","Microsoft Learn (fileapi LockFile): 'If a process terminates with a portion of a file locked ... the locks are unlocked by the operating system. However, the time it takes ... depends upon available system resources.'","GitHub microsoft/Windows-Containers#37: 'LockFile lock is not released by OS when process' exits under some configs","ABSENCE: plan does not state how the staleness proof tolerates non-instantaneous OS release, nor whether the native test allows for release latency"],"finding":"The design's load-bearing claim that the Windows exclusive leg is released promptly when the holder dies is not resolved against the primitive's documented behavior: Microsoft's LockFile/LockFileEx documentation states the OS unlock on process termination is NOT immediate ('the time it takes ... depends upon available system resources'), and a real report (microsoft/Windows-Containers#37) shows a case where the lock survives process exit. The staleness proof (requirement 3) and the native release-on-death test (requirement 5) implicitly assume prompt/reliable release.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f7dad0622a73a8197","impact":0.55,"is_primary":null,"location":"What a correct fix requires, items 1 & 3; AC 'The Windows primitive's cross-process exclusivity and release-on-process-death are proved empirically'","norm_id":"n01e592b97a02f241","priority":0.3575,"reason":"default-advisory","recalled":false,"scenarios":["A Windows holder crashes; the OS has not yet released the byte-range lock; rebar's staleness proof concludes the lock is still held and blocks a legitimate reclaim, or races on the timing the native test asserts.","The native windows-latest test kills the child and immediately checks the parent can acquire, but OS release lags, producing a flaky RED that reopens the story per the plan's own gate."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"","tier":"LLM","validity":0.65,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"insufficient","cited_reference_accurate":"insufficient","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"yes","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"no","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"yes"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"module","debt_impact":"medium","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"underspecified_certification","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"medium","reversibility":"moderate","silent_vs_self_revealing":"self_revealing","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"low"}}},{"block_threshold":0.9,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Resolve which Windows primitive the leg uses (msvcrt.locking vs LockFileEx) and specify its exact surface — for msvcrt.locking, the byte-range length locked on the possibly-zero-length .ticket-write.lock file — rather than naming two candidates with differing semantics.","cohort":["T3"],"criteria":["T3"],"decision":"advisory","decision_margin":-0.6052,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan quote: 'msvcrt.locking(fd, LK_NBLCK, n) (or LockFileEx via ctypes)'","msvcrt/msvcrt.locking/LK_NBLCK are UNRESOLVED on the POSIX review host (not proof of absence, but the specific surface is unconfirmed here)","msvcrt.locking locks an n-byte region starting at the current file position; the plan's 'n' is unspecified and .ticket-write.lock is opened O_CREAT (potentially zero-length), which would make the region parameter load-bearing","EVIDENCE CLASS: Partially-verified — the integration (a Windows exclusive leg) is achievable in principle, but the specific primitive/surface chosen is unconfirmed"],"finding":"The plan names two alternative Windows exclusive-lock primitives with materially different surfaces (msvcrt.locking(fd, LK_NBLCK, n) vs LockFileEx via ctypes) but does not resolve which, nor confirm the specific surface detail that msvcrt.locking is a byte-RANGE lock requiring a nonzero n-byte region on the .ticket-write.lock file (which acquire() opens with O_CREAT and may be zero-length). The exact API surface and its byte-range parameterization are Unverified.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f34f2942e082abc08","impact":0.536,"is_primary":null,"location":"Section 'What a correct fix requires', requirement 1","norm_id":"n15be18e079ad0d45","priority":0.2948,"reason":"default-advisory","recalled":false,"scenarios":["Implementer picks msvcrt.locking with LK_NBLCK against a zero-length lock file and the byte-range lock behaves differently than flock's whole-file advisory lock","The two candidate primitives differ in release-on-death or cross-process visibility guarantees, so the choice silently changes whether the fcntl_held=True precondition holds"],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"","tier":"LLM","validity":0.55,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"insufficient","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"yes","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"no","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"no","severity_claim_justified":"yes"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"medium","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"self_revealing","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"medium"}}},{"block_threshold":0.9,"blocking_enabled":false,"checklist_item":"- [ ] State the failure-disposition contract for the leg selector: classify contended-refusal (retryable/transient) distinctly from no-leg-available (fatal/permanent), and confirm the raise on the no-leg arm carries the fatal disposition rather than being mistaken for contention.","cohort":["E3","E5","G5","T6","T9","necessity","project.engine-layer-enforcement","project.failure-disposition-contract","project.portability"],"criteria":["project.failure-disposition-contract"],"decision":"dropped","decision_margin":-0.834,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["'write_lock_is_busy (lock.py:254), an advisory zero-deadline probe that fails OPEN'","'with NO exclusive leg available, acquire() raises and never creates .ticket-write.lock.d'","The plan introduces a leg-selection fallback chain (fcntl absent -> Windows leg -> none) but no disposition word (retryable/transient vs fatal/permanent) is attached to either the contended-refusal arm or the no-leg arm."],"finding":"The plan alters lock-acquisition failure semantics (the fcntl -> Windows-leg -> no-leg selector chain) but never states the disposition contract in explicit retryable/fatal terms: what does the caller surface when a live holder REFUSES the Windows leg (contended) versus when NO leg is available at all (the acquire()-raises case)? The former is a transient/retryable contention; the latter is a permanent/fatal configuration error, and the two must not be conflated by a single raise.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f375787f2af5e4eaf","impact":0.264,"is_primary":null,"location":"'What a correct fix requires' item 2 and AC line 3 ('with NO exclusive leg available, acquire() raises')","norm_id":"n8887313366deb764","priority":0.066,"reason":"veto:absence-refuted","recalled":false,"scenarios":["On Windows, msvcrt.locking refuses because a live sibling holds the write lock: the caller should see a retryable/transient busy disposition, but the plan does not say so.","In a stripped environment where neither fcntl nor msvcrt exists, acquire() raises — this should be a fatal/permanent disposition, distinct from contention, but the plan states only 'raises' with no classification, risking a retry loop against an unfixable condition."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"Add a 'Failure-disposition contract' note: a Windows-leg refusal by a live holder surfaces the SAME retryable/busy disposition POSIX flock contention does; total absence of any exclusive primitive surfaces a distinct fatal/permanent error at acquire() (never retried), and this fatal arm never masks a concurrent retryable contention on the same path.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.25,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"no","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"no","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"no","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"no","severity_claim_justified":"no"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"low","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"self_revealing","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"low"}}}],"impact_model_version":"plan-v5","material_fingerprint":"369bff3fbceaf74e","material_parts":{"children":["647ea2107ca5072f",0],"description":["2bd869dd144b5508",7981],"description_boxes":["399141be1d30ac26",0],"file_impact":["d256a12a7c5196fb",5],"ticket_id":["c9dff74740249298",19]},"metrics":{"cache_read_tokens":332978,"cache_write_tokens":130365,"claim_path":"no-llm/no-network (structural; the fast claim check is a local HMAC verify)","det_ms":14458.3,"input_tokens":485046,"llm_calls":27,"llm_ms":269114.2,"output_tokens":25969,"precheck":{"cited_anchor_warning":false},"total_ms":309426.9,"verify_requests":1},"model":"bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-6","provider_provenance":{"candidates":["bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-6","anthropic:claude-sonnet-4-6"],"capabilities":{"native_structured_output":true,"prompt_cache_style":"bedrock","supports_temperature":true,"supports_thinking":true,"web_access":"off"},"endpoint_host":null,"model":"bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-6","provider":"bedrock","